[
    {
        "content": "<p>It seems surprising that neither simp, grind, nor aesop can prove this easy-looking fact:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Mathlib</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">P</span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Bool</span><span class=\"o\">):</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">P</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∅</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∀</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">¬</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">P</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">try?</span><span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"c1\">-- fails</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>On the other hand, if <code>a</code> belongs to a set rather than a type then <code>simp</code> proves it:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">S</span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Set</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">P</span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Bool</span><span class=\"o\">):</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∈</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">S</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">P</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∅</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∀</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∈</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">S</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">¬</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">P</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">simp</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>That is due to this simplication lemma in <code>Mathlib/Data/Set/Basic.lean</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kd\">@[</span><span class=\"n\">simp</span><span class=\"kd\">]</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">theorem</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">sep_eq_empty_iff_mem_false</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∈</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∅</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">↔</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∀</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∈</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">¬</span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">simp_rw</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Set</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">ext_iff</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">mem_sep_iff</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">mem_empty_iff_false</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">iff_false</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">not_and</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>I'm a bit unsure about how to interpret the situation.  Should Mathlib arguably contain a similar simplication lemma of the form <code>{ x : T | p x } = ∅ ↔ ∀ x : T, ¬p x</code> ?  It seems surprising that it doesn't, since a comprehension with <code>x : T</code> seems just as likely as one with <code>x ∈ s</code>.  Or am I missing something here, i.e. is there some easy way to get simp, grind or aesop to prove my first statement above?</p>",
        "id": 631668393,
        "sender_full_name": "Adam Dingle",
        "timestamp": 1791987927
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I realized that in my examples above I should really be using <code>Prop</code> rather than <code>Bool</code>.</p>\n<p>With that change, <code>try?</code> can prove my first statement:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">P</span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Prop</span><span class=\"o\">):</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">P</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∅</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∀</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">¬</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">P</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">try?</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>It suggests</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"n\">Try</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">this</span><span class=\"o\">:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">apply</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">·</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">intros</span><span class=\"bp\">;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">expose_names</span><span class=\"bp\">;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">exact</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">of_eq_false</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">congrFun</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">h</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>But grind and aesop still cannot prove it.  It would be nice to know if that is somehow possible.</p>",
        "id": 631676835,
        "sender_full_name": "Adam Dingle",
        "timestamp": 1791989530
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>This would be solved by giving <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/Data/Set/Basic.html#Set.eq_empty_iff_forall_notMem\">Set.eq_empty_iff_forall_notMem</a> the <code>simp</code> attribute. (then it could be solved by <code>simp</code>)</p>\n<p>Occasionally a hypothesis or goal of the form <code>s = ∅</code> is somewhat annoying to me, so i think changing it would be  worth it. But maybe there are other considerations why this is not the case.<br>\nEDIT: Thinking about it this would break a lot of other simp lemmas, so maybe not after all...</p>",
        "id": 631721268,
        "sender_full_name": "Felix Pernegger",
        "timestamp": 1791999113
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"780789\">@Felix Pernegger</span> Aha - I had not discovered that theorem.  Indeed, using it we can prove my example using <code>simp</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">P</span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Prop</span><span class=\"o\">):</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">P</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∅</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∀</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">¬</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">P</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">simp</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Set</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">eq_empty_iff_forall_notMem</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>That's great.  Thanks for the suggestion.</p>\n<p>I agree it seems like it would be nice to give this theorem the <code>simp</code> attribute, however you wrote that it would break things.  How about this alternative?</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kd\">@[</span><span class=\"n\">simp</span><span class=\"kd\">]</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">theorem</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">eq_empty_helper</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Prop</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∅</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">↔</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∀</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">¬</span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"k\">by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"gr\">sorry</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>(I wrote <code>by sorry</code> since I haven't bothered to prove it, though I think it wouldn't be hard.  I also haven't given it a name following Lean's naming conventions, since I'm not sure how to do that for this theorem.)</p>\n<p>If this theorem existed and had the <code>simp</code> attribute, then <code>simp</code> would prove my example automatically.  However would that also break other simp lemmas?</p>",
        "id": 631736310,
        "sender_full_name": "Adam Dingle",
        "timestamp": 1792002492
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I think you <code>eq_empty_helper</code> could potentially be a good addition (dont worry about naming now). I <em>think</em> transparency settings for <code>simp</code>make this relatively unproblematic / should not break (too) much (as I think <code>Set.univ</code>isnt affeted and empty set), but best is just to try. :)</p>",
        "id": 631750655,
        "sender_full_name": "Felix Pernegger",
        "timestamp": 1792005923
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"687151\">Adam Dingle</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/113489-new-members/topic/any.20way.20to.20get.20grind.20or.20aesop.20to.20prove.20this.20easy.20fact.3F/near/631676835\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>But grind and aesop still cannot prove it.  It would be nice to know if that is somehow possible.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Also FYi aesop is kind of outdated; I wouldnt worry about it</p>",
        "id": 631750908,
        "sender_full_name": "Felix Pernegger",
        "timestamp": 1792005982
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Felix, thanks for your thoughts.  By the way I've found that aesop can prove some assertions that grind cannot, so I think it's still a useful tool to have in the toolchest.</p>",
